  <Fragment slot="content">
    <section class="fraction-content">
      <h2>Fraction Calculator – Solve, Simplify and Convert Fractions</h2>
      <p>
        The <strong>Fraction Calculator</strong> is an all-in-one arithmetic and algebraic fraction solver. Whether you need to add, subtract, multiply, or divide fractions, calculate with mixed numbers, convert decimals to fractions, simplify fractions to lowest terms, or perform advanced <strong>partial fraction decomposition</strong>, this calculator provides instant results with clear step-by-step solutions.
      </p>

      <h2>What Is a Fraction Calculator?</h2>
      <p>
        A <strong>fraction</strong> represents a part of a whole number or a ratio between two quantities. It consists of:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Numerator:</strong> The top number, indicating how many equal parts are taken.</li>
        <li><strong>Denominator:</strong> The bottom number, indicating into how many equal parts the whole is divided (the denominator can never be zero).</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How to Add and Subtract Fractions</h2>
      <p>
        To add or subtract fractions with different denominators, you must first convert them to equivalent fractions with a <strong>Least Common Denominator (LCD)</strong>:
      </p>
      <div class="my-4 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-100/80 p-4 font-mono text-sm dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
        <div>Addition Rule: (a/b) + (c/d) = (a·d + b·c) / (b·d)</div>
        <div class="mt-1">Subtraction Rule: (a/b) − (c/d) = (a·d − b·c) / (b·d)</div>
      </div>
      <p>
        <strong>Example:</strong> Adding <code>1/2 + 3/4</code>. The common denominator is <code>4</code>.
        <br />
        <code>1/2 = 2/4</code>.
        <br />
        <code>2/4 + 3/4 = 5/4 = 1 1/4 (1.25)</code>.
      </p>

      <h2>How to Multiply and Divide Fractions</h2>
      <p>
        Fraction multiplication and division do not require finding a common denominator:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Multiplication:</strong> Multiply the numerators together and the denominators together: <code>(a/b) × (c/d) = (a·c) / (b·d)</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Division:</strong> Multiply the first fraction by the reciprocal (flipped form) of the second fraction: <code>(a/b) ÷ (c/d) = (a/b) × (d/c) = (a·d) / (b·c)</code>.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Mixed Fraction Calculator</h2>
      <p>
        A <strong>mixed number</strong> (such as <code>2 1/3</code>) combines a whole integer with a proper fraction. To perform calculations, our <strong>mixed fraction calculator</strong> converts each mixed fraction into an improper fraction:
        <br />
        <code>2 1/3 = (2 × 3 + 1) / 3 = 7/3</code>.
        <br />
        After evaluating the arithmetic, the resulting improper fraction is converted back to a simplified mixed number.
      </p>

      <h2>Decimal to Fraction Calculator</h2>
      <p>
        Terminating decimals can be converted into exact fractions by placing the decimal digits over the corresponding power of 10 (such as <code>0.75 = 75/100 = 3/4</code>). For recurring decimals, continued fraction algorithms find the closest rational approximation matching your desired precision.
      </p>

      <h2>How to Simplify a Fraction</h2>
      <p>
        A fraction is in its simplest form when its numerator and denominator have no common factors other than 1. To simplify a fraction, divide both the numerator and denominator by their <strong>Greatest Common Divisor (GCD)</strong>:
        <br />
        <code>24/36 ➔ GCD(24, 36) = 12 ➔ (24÷12) / (36÷12) = 2/3</code>.
      </p>

      <h2>Partial Fraction Calculator</h2>
      <p>
        In calculus, integration, and differential equations, <strong>partial fraction decomposition</strong> is used to break down complex rational algebraic fractions into sums of simpler distinct linear or quadratic terms:
        <br />
        <code>(3x + 5) / ((x - 1)(x + 2)) = [8/3 / (x - 1)] + [1/3 / (x + 2)]</code>.
      </p>

      <h2>Calculator-Style Fraction Input</h2>
      <p>
        Many students and engineers search for workflows familiar to hardware calculators such as a <em>casio fx-55 plus fraction calculator</em>. Our Expression Parser tab allows direct entry of compound fraction expressions like <code>(1/2 + 3/4) * 2</code> with instant algebraic evaluation.
      </p>
      <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 italic">
        * Notice: This calculator is an independent online educational tool and is not affiliated with or endorsed by Casio Computer Co., Ltd.
      </p>

